Day 13 – Rolling Updates & Rollbacks 🔄
1. Why Rolling Updates?

Suppose our application is running Version 1:

Pod 1 → v1
Pod 2 → v1
Pod 3 → v1

Now developers release Version 2.

We don't want to do:

❌ Delete all v1 Pods
❌ Then create all v2 Pods

Because this can cause downtime.

Instead, Kubernetes gradually replaces v1 Pods with v2 Pods.

v1  v1  v1
 ↓
v2  v1  v1
 ↓
v2  v2  v1
 ↓
v2  v2  v2
Remember:

Rolling Update = replace old Pods with new Pods in stages.

"Gradually" means in steps, not necessarily slowly.

2. Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod

A Deployment manages the rollout.

Deployment
    ↓
ReplicaSet
    ↓
Pods

Suppose Version 1 is running:

Deployment
    ↓
ReplicaSet v1
    ↓
v1   v1   v1

When we update to Version 2, Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet.

It does NOT convert the old ReplicaSet.

Deployment
    │
    ├── ReplicaSet v1
    │      └── v1 Pods
    │
    └── ReplicaSet v2
           └── v2 Pods

During the update, Kubernetes decreases the number of Pods in v1 and increases the number of Pods in v2.

3. What happens during a Rolling Update?

Suppose:

replicas: 3

Initially:

v1   v1   v1

During the update:

v1   v1
v2   v2

Then:

v1
v2   v2   v2

Finally:

v2   v2   v2

The old ReplicaSet becomes:

DESIRED = 0

and the new ReplicaSet becomes:

DESIRED = 3

The old ReplicaSet itself can still remain in the cluster for revision history.

4. Rollback

Suppose Version 2 has a bug.

Current state:

v2   v2   v2

We want to return to the previous working version:

v1   v1   v1

This is called a Rollback.

Because Kubernetes retains the previous Deployment revision, it can bring the old version back.

v1 → v2
     ↓
   Problem
     ↓
  Rollback
     ↓
v1
Command:
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app

Think:

rollout undo = go back to the previous revision

5. Rollout History

To see the Deployment's revisions:

kubectl rollout history deployment/my-app

Example:

REVISION  CHANGE-CAUSE
1
2
3

Think:

history = show me the Deployment's versions/revisions.

6. Rollout Status

To check whether the update has completed:

kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app

Example:

deployment "my-app" successfully rolled out

Think:

status = tell me whether the rollout is finished.

7. Update Strategy

A Deployment determines how the Pods should be replaced using an update strategy.

There are two important strategies for Day 13.

RollingUpdate
strategy:
  type: RollingUpdate

Old Pods are gradually replaced with new Pods.

v1 v1 v1
 ↓
v2 v1 v1
 ↓
v2 v2 v1
 ↓
v2 v2 v2
Advantage

✅ Helps maintain availability during updates.

Recreate
strategy:
  type: Recreate

Kubernetes first removes the old Pods:

v1 v1 v1
 ↓
(no Pods)
 ↓
v2 v2 v2
Disadvantage

❌ There can be downtime between the old and new versions.

8. RollingUpdate vs Recreate
Strategy	What happens
RollingUpdate	Old Pods gradually replaced
Recreate	Old Pods removed first, then new Pods created

For normal application updates, RollingUpdate is commonly preferred when you want to avoid downtime.

9. Revision History

Old ReplicaSets are retained for rollback/history.

By default:

revisionHistoryLimit: 10

This means Kubernetes keeps up to 10 old revisions.

It is not a time limit.

So don't think:

"Kubernetes keeps v1 for 10 days."

Think:

"Kubernetes keeps a certain number of old revisions."

10. Important Hands-on Commands
Deploy
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
Check Deployment
kubectl get deployment
Check ReplicaSets
kubectl get rs
Check Pods
kubectl get pods
Check rollout status
kubectl rollout status deployment/my-app
Check rollout history
kubectl rollout history deployment/my-app
Rollback
kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app
